<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Filth (RimWorld: ThingDefs_Misc/Filth.xml). Its own directory under Defs/, which DefLoader.AddDirectory
    picks up recursively, so nothing shared had to change to ship it.

    Every filth def is category Filth, which ListerThings already indexes as ThingRequestGroup.Filth and
    ThingGrid already allows to coexist with whatever else is in the cell. altitudeLayer Filth likewise
    already existed in ThingEnums - the scaffolding for this was in place before the class was.

    Numbers below are SimWorld's own, not sourced from RimWorld's content: maxThickness 3 is RimWorld's own
    documented default, but cleaningWorkToReduceThickness, the disappear ranges and the cleanliness offsets
    are not values this port could source. The filth tests therefore pin trends and bands - thicker filth
    takes longer to clean, blood goes away on its own and dirt does not, more filth means a dirtier room -
    rather than any literal here (CLAUDE.md).

    Beauty is the route filth takes to mood (system: needs.beauty, which landed after this file). Each pile
    carries a negative Beauty stat, BeautyUtility averages it over the cells a citizen perceives, and the
    Beauty ThoughtDef turns that into mood - RimWorld's own route, where every filth def carries a Beauty
    entry exactly like this. It replaces the FilthyRoom situational thought this module shipped with, which
    read room cleanliness directly because nothing implemented IEnvironmentSampler at the time and whose own
    doc said to delete it the day one landed rather than count filth against mood twice.

    The values are SimWorld's own, like the cleanliness ones above, and ordered to agree with them: animal
    filth is the worst of the three, dirt the mildest. BeautyTests pins that ordering and the band, not these
    numbers.

    useHitPoints is false on all three: filth is not something you can shoot, and leaving hit points on would
    let the repair work giver and the explosion damage sweep both treat it as a Thing with a health bar.
  -->

  <ThingDef Name="FilthBase" Abstract="True">
    <category>Filth</category>
    <thingClass>SimWorld.Filth.Filth</thingClass>
    <altitudeLayer>Filth</altitudeLayer>
    <useHitPoints>false</useHitPoints>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="FilthBase">
    <defName>Filth_Dirt</defName>
    <label>dirt</label>
    <description>Loose dirt, tracked in on somebody's boots or worked up off a bare floor.</description>
    <statBases>
      <Cleanliness>-2</Cleanliness>
      <Beauty>-8</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <filth>
      <maxThickness>3</maxThickness>
      <cleaningWorkToReduceThickness>40</cleaningWorkToReduceThickness>
      <!--
        The inverted TerrainDef.generatedFilth (see FilthProperties.sourceTerrains). Every bare, unfloored
        terrain a pawn can walk on is here; water and the stone/asphalt roads are not, which is what makes
        laying a floor the thing that stops a room getting dirty by itself.
      -->
      <sourceTerrains>
        <li>Soil</li>
        <li>SoilRich</li>
        <li>Gravel</li>
        <li>Sand</li>
        <li>Mud</li>
        <li>MarshyTerrain</li>
        <li>StreetDirtPath</li>
        <li>StreetDirtRoad</li>
      </sourceTerrains>
    </filth>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="FilthBase">
    <defName>Filth_Blood</defName>
    <label>blood</label>
    <description>A splash of blood.</description>
    <statBases>
      <Cleanliness>-3</Cleanliness>
      <Beauty>-12</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <filth>
      <maxThickness>3</maxThickness>
      <cleaningWorkToReduceThickness>40</cleaningWorkToReduceThickness>
      <!-- Dries and flakes away on its own; dirt does not. Rolled per pile, so a massacre's worth of blood
           does not all vanish on the same tick. -->
      <disappearsInDays>2~5</disappearsInDays>
      <canFilthAttach>false</canFilthAttach>
    </filth>
  </ThingDef>

  <ThingDef ParentName="FilthBase">
    <defName>Filth_AnimalFilth</defName>
    <label>animal filth</label>
    <description>Droppings left by an animal that got indoors.</description>
    <statBases>
      <Cleanliness>-4</Cleanliness>
      <Beauty>-16</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <filth>
      <maxThickness>2</maxThickness>
      <cleaningWorkToReduceThickness>60</cleaningWorkToReduceThickness>
      <disappearsInDays>6~10</disappearsInDays>
    </filth>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
